<div class="case-explainer">
  <section id="brief" aria-labelledby="brief-title">
    <h2 class="sr-only" id="brief-title">The brief and the scale</h2>

    <figure>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="flow-node">
          <h3>Flat roster</h3>
          <p>A spreadsheet of 1,886 participation rows across film, theatre, music, digital, and live events.</p>
          <span class="node-tag">Only 186 rows carried a single profile link.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>scrape &amp; screen</span>
          <b>→</b>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-node is-accent">
          <h3>130M+ rows</h3>
          <p>Bulk dumps, state registries, streaming catalogs, and live web scrapers mirrored into local SQLite FTS5 indexes.</p>
          <span class="node-tag">70 canonical sources behind 20 modules.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>aggregate</span>
          <b>→</b>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-node">
          <h3>Market impact</h3>
          <p>6,739 credited works, verified audience reach, deduplicated box office, ratings, Excel workbooks, and a 29-slide deck.</p>
          <span class="node-tag">91.8% of works carry a direct URL.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Manual search could not answer an industry-scale question in eight days. The solution was to pull the external catalogs onto local disk, stream millions of credit rows against a normalized person index, and scrape the open web for the long tail.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="section-copy">
      <p>
        Eight days before an executive review, a national talent competition needed to measure the cumulative industry footprint of its finalists over seven seasons. The team had started collecting credits by hand and asked if AI could extract the rest from the internet: every film, series, music release, theatre production, game, and festival project created since 2020, along with audience reach, box office revenue, and average ratings.
      </p>
      <p>
        On input, I received a flat spreadsheet of 1,886 participation rows covering 1,513 unique professionals across five creative sectors: film, theatre, music, digital, and events. Only 186 rows contained a social link; the rest held a name, a birth date, a city, a discipline, and a season. Searching 1,513 people one by one through live web queries would have hit rate limits on the first afternoon and drowned in namesake noise.
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead of treating the request as manual lookup, I built a web-scraping and data-engineering pipeline. Over eight days of logged work, 585 Python scripts and 20 source modules ingested and screened more than 130 million records across encyclopedic dumps, state registries, streaming APIs, and authenticated browser sessions, resolving 6,739 credited works and 2,735 verified professional profiles.
      </p>
      <p class="measurement-note">
        Client identity, participant names, and individual project titles are withheld by agreement. Every pipeline metric, source class, and throughput figure below comes from the production logs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

