  <section id="scale" aria-labelledby="scale-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="scale-title">We pulled the internet onto local disk before matching a single name.</h2>
      <p>
        Live APIs are built for single lookups, not for cross-referencing 1,513 people across four name representations each. Public film and music APIs cap requests at 200 to 500 calls a day. To run at web scale without quota walls, the pipeline first downloaded bulk dumps and open registries, converted them into local SQLite databases with FTS5 full-text indexes, and streamed multi-gigabyte archives in constant memory.
      </p>
    </div>

    <dl class="metric-strip">
      <div>
        <dt>102.6M</dt>
        <dd>IMDb credit rows filtered in a single streaming pass across 15.8M names and 12.9M titles</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>22.4M</dt>
        <dd>Artist and creator records indexed locally across Discogs, Yandex Music, TMDB, and Kinopoisk dumps</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>56,598/s</dt>
        <dd>Rows screened per second against the person index with a flat 38 MB RAM ceiling</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>70</dt>
        <dd>Canonical data sources unified from 193 raw labels across registries, dumps, and scrapers</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <figure>
      <dl class="throughput">
        <div>
          <dt>IMDb official TSV datasets (1.4 GB compressed → 5.0 GB SQLite index)</dt>
          <dd><strong>102.6M</strong> credit rows screened in 150 s, episodes rolled up to parent series</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Kinopoisk consolidated JSONL dump (810 MB gzip / 3.8 GB uncompressed)</dt>
          <dd><strong>1,060,836</strong> films streamed line by line at <strong>38 MB</strong> constant RAM</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>TMDB daily person export (5,051,606 person records)</dt>
          <dd><strong>56,598 rows/s</strong> streaming token-boundary screen in constant memory</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Yandex Music local SQLite catalog (967 MB indexed database)</dt>
          <dd><strong>6,039,445</strong> artists with sub-millisecond B-tree lookups + live credits API</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Discogs &amp; MusicBrainz open music encyclopedias (477.6 MB XML dump)</dt>
          <dd><strong>10,284,281</strong> artist records streamed via <code>iterparse</code> in <strong>288 s</strong></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>National film, certificate, theatre, and grant registries (MKRF, UNF, FPG, PFKI)</dt>
          <dd><strong>380,000+</strong> official release certificates, repertoire plays, and grant records</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Steam Games dataset (1 GB JSON, 148,909 titles via <code>ijson</code> stream)</dt>
          <dd><strong>16 s</strong> full ingest for player reach and review volume enrichment</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <figcaption>
        Every bulk source was reduced to a local SQLite mirror or constant-memory stream (<code>ijson</code>, <code>iterparse</code> with immediate element clearing, or <code>gzip</code> line generators). Local FTS5 queries run in under 1 millisecond and cost zero API quota.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="scraping" aria-labelledby="scraping-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="scraping-title">When bulk dumps ended, browser relays and protocol tricks took over.</h2>
      <p>
        Bulk dumps cover encyclopedias and state registries, but creative professionals publish across dozens of live platforms: Vimeo showreels, Behance and Dprofile portfolios, ArtStation, VKontakte, Telegram channels, rights societies, and legacy cinema portals. Each platform blocks automated collection differently: strict daily quotas, Cloudflare Turnstile, DDOS-GUARD image captchas, JA3/JA4 TLS fingerprinting, or 401 walls on unauthenticated endpoints.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <div class="source-head" aria-hidden="true">
        <span>Target layer</span>
        <span>What we extracted</span>
        <span>How the pipeline bypassed the wall</span>
      </div>
      <div class="source-table">
        <div class="source-row">
          <h3>Vimeo &amp; Next.js state JWTs</h3>
          <p>353 video works across 30 verified channels and 43 finalists, plus full crew lists that exposed cross-disciplinary collaborations.</p>
          <p class="source-fail">Extracted the public client JWT embedded inside the Next.js state of any profile page, then queried the internal catalog API at 0.2 s per channel.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="source-row">
          <h3>Dprofile &amp; Кино-Театр.РУ behind auth and captchas</h3>
          <p>650+ design cases, unmasked contacts, and legacy filmographies that returned HTTP 401 or image captchas to headless browsers.</p>
          <p class="source-fail">Attached Playwright MCP Extension to a live authenticated desktop Chrome session and ran batched in-page <code>fetch</code> loops inside the origin.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="source-row">
          <h3>Rights society (RAO) &amp; federal portals</h3>
          <p>314 registered musical scores, stage designs, and scripts across 1,532 scanned names, plus 37 subsidized cinema PDFs.</p>
          <p class="source-fail">WAF dropped Python HTTP clients via TLS fingerprinting; routed requests through the OS native Schannel stack (<code>curl</code>) at 100% pass rate and parsed PDFs at 340 pages/s.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="source-row">
          <h3>Kinopoisk APIs &amp; EAIS box office</h3>
          <p>Box office revenue, ticketed attendance, and viewer ratings across commercial theatrical releases and streaming originals.</p>
          <p class="source-fail">Paired a two-tier SQLite disk cache (<code>MAX_SAFE_LIVE_CALLS = 175</code>) with direct JSON lookups by state certificate number at 0.05 s per film.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="source-row">
          <h3>VKontakte, Telegram, Exa, Apify &amp; OSINT</h3>
          <p>3,880 profiles across 32 platforms, 660 works from a 7,060-message Telegram archive, and 240 Tilda results pages parsed by coordinates.</p>
          <p class="source-fail">Used server-side <code>VKScript execute</code> batches (25 profiles/call), a 35,000-member community index, Exa semantic search, Maigret, and GHunt.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Network routing was split by geography: international platforms ran through a transparent VLESS Reality TUN adapter (<code>happ-xray</code>) or Apify cloud actors, while domestic registries ran direct to avoid geo-blocks.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="section-copy">
      <p>
        One of the highest-yield techniques was the Cross-Credits Graph on Vimeo and YouTube. Video descriptions rarely name just the channel owner: they list the director, cinematographer, editor, colorist, sound designer, and VFX artist. By parsing full crew blocks from 30 verified channels and matching every line back against the 1,513-person index, a single scraped video simultaneously verified works for multiple finalists across different disciplines.
      </p>
      <p>
        Equally important was knowing when to stop scraping a dead source. When a legacy cinema portal blocked the IP after 100 requests and only 0.7% of 698 scanned finalists were present on the platform, we used the live browser relay only to finish the 5 pending hits rather than building a proxy farm for an empty well.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="filtering" aria-labelledby="filtering-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="filtering-title">Matching 1,513 people against 130 million rows is a filtering problem.</h2>
      <p>
        Scraping the data is only half the engineering; keeping false positives out of the database is the other half. Thousands of people share the same first and last name, and 441 of the 1,513 participants were juniors aged 14 to 17 with thin public footprints, hidden birth years, or stage names. Raw name matching produced 97% noise: only 2.8% of name matches on IMDb and 4.3% on MobyGames belonged to the actual finalist.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every enricher therefore enforced a strict multi-factor filter stack before a candidate record could touch the database.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <div class="gate-stack">
        <div class="gate">
          <h3>Bilingual Cyrillic + Latin transliteration and stage names</h3>
          <p>
            Every person was queried across four representations: Cyrillic forms, bidirectional Latin transliteration (<code>-iy/-y</code>, <code>kh/h</code>, <code>zh/j</code>), social handles, and verified stage names. Adding Latin search uncovered 400+ releases for media composers who publish exclusively in English.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="gate">
          <h3>Token-set intersection, never substring matching</h3>
          <p>
            Substring checks matched a German specialist named <em>Anna Simon</em> inside the Russian surname <em>Simonova</em>. The matcher requires exact token-set intersection with normalized Slavic gender endings (<code>-ov/-ova</code>, <code>-in/-ina</code>, <code>-sky/-skaya</code>).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="gate">
          <h3>Anti-Senior chronological filter</h3>
          <p>
            The dominant error is not a random stranger; it is an older industry veteran with the same name and decades of credits. Any candidate with a work released before the finalist’s birth year plus 13 (or 16 for adults) is disqualified in full.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="gate">
          <h3>Patronymic verification and institutional exclusions</h3>
          <p>
            Where a registry or track credit carried a conflicting patronymic, the match was dropped immediately. Context regexes also stripped institutional false positives such as film schools or foundations named after a famous surname.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Retrieval and verification remained separate gates. Even when a hard target was extracted through an authenticated human browser session, three of five retrieved candidates were still rejected on birth-date mismatch.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="state-grid">
      <div>
        <h3>Verified &amp; kept</h3>
        <p class="state-strong">6,739</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Active credited works across film (2,985), portfolio cases (1,246), music tracks &amp; albums (1,435), animation (426), theatre (344), digital art, and games.</li>
          <li>1,437 of 1,513 people (95.0%) enriched with verified profiles or works; 1,034 have confirmed projects on file.</li>
          <li>6,186 works (91.8%) linked to a direct source URL.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Filtered out</h3>
        <p class="state-strong">1.87M+</p>
        <ul>
          <li>1,304,911 senior namesake records and 569,429 unrelated cast/crew rows rejected in the Kinopoisk dump pass alone.</li>
          <li>648 false namesakes rejected on TMDB and 160 works from 3 senior namesakes blocked at the rights registry.</li>
          <li>66 people left honestly as not found rather than padded with unverified guesses.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="market-model" aria-labelledby="market-model-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="market-model-title">Raw rows are not market metrics.</h2>
      <p>
        The client needed headline numbers for a seven-year impact report across five creative sectors: total projects, active specialists, audience reach, and financial volume. Turning 6,739 scraped works into numbers that survive executive scrutiny required solving three analytical traps: double-counting collaborative blockbusters, mixing project budgets with personal income, and missing release years.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="flow-node">
          <h3>Deduplicated box office</h3>
          <p>When two finalists worked on the same feature film, summing per-person rows inflated box office by 56% (16.4B vs 10.48B RUB).</p>
          <span class="node-tag">Rule: group by work, take MAX, never SUM.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>separate</span>
          <b>→</b>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-node is-accent">
          <h3>Two-layer financials</h3>
          <p>Gross market output (box office, stage budgets, grants, game capitalization: 14B+ RUB) is separated from direct specialist income and royalties (1.2B–1.8B RUB).</p>
          <span class="node-tag">Never mix film gross with composer royalties.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>syndicate</span>
          <b>→</b>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-node">
          <h3>Multi-contour reach</h3>
          <p>Combines deduplicated theatrical admissions (11.0M viewers), 6-year syndicated music streaming + soundtrack cinema reach (17.4M), and domestic vs global Steam players.</p>
          <span class="node-tag">36.8M viewer votes across 1,956 rated works.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Every macro metric in the client report is backed by a SQL view and an explicit formula printed on the slide, separating the 2020–2026 competition window (5,259 works; 2,449 in cinema) from full-career retrospectives (6,739 works).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="section-copy">
      <p>
        Because the client specifically asked for works released since 2020, undated rows were a direct loss of reporting coverage. Initially, 1,340 active works (26.0%) lacked a release year. I built a seven-stage date-recovery cascade ordered from free local indexes to quota-capped APIs: local ministry registry title matching, title year regexes, Vimeo v2 upload timestamps, Yandex Music track credits, PoiskKino searches, Kinopoisk Unofficial keyword lookups, and studio date cards.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cascade recovered 529 release years, cutting undated works from 26.0% to 15.8%, and then stopped where the data genuinely ends: 165 works live in a rights registry that has no date column at all, and 22 one-word short films cannot be disambiguated from older films with the same title.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="boundary-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Null is unknown, never zero.</strong> Where a film had fewer votes than a platform’s publication threshold (138 titles) or no public box office, the database stores <code>NULL</code> rather than <code>0</code> so averages and medians (7.20 mean, 7.4 median across film and animation) are never dragged down by missing data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="agents" aria-labelledby="agents-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="agents-title">Three agent platforms wrote 55,000 lines of scraper code. I ran the architecture.</h2>
      <p>
        One person directed the entire eight-day sprint in natural language without writing SQL or scraper boilerplate by hand. Three agent platforms worked in sequential rotation over a single project state: Google Antigravity executed 58 logged milestones, OpenCode ran 34 data-hygiene and dump-streaming passes, and OpenAI Codex handled legal redlines, transactional audits, and the anonymized export (4 steps).
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <div class="agent-flow">
        <div class="agent-step">
          <h3>Single regulation</h3>
          <p>One 348 KB <code>AGENTS.md</code> file holds the schema, 32 source playbooks, 59 success patterns, and 75 failure patterns. No model-specific copies.</p>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </div>
        <div class="agent-step">
          <h3>Database queue</h3>
          <p>Work batches are pulled from SQLite views (<code>pending</code>, <code>enriched</code> with zero works, <code>needs_review</code>). No agent invents its own scope.</p>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </div>
        <div class="agent-step">
          <h3>Read-only subagents</h3>
          <p>Parallel subagents fan out across APIs, caches, and browsers to harvest candidates and return structured JSON without touching SQLite.</p>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </div>
        <div class="agent-step">
          <h3>Atomic write &amp; log</h3>
          <p>The parent agent takes a dated pre-flight backup, writes one transaction, recomputes active views, and appends to the 96-step <code>PROGRESS.md</code> log.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Strict single-writer orchestration allowed 20+ parallel research subagents to run without SQLite lock collisions or state corruption.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="lesson-grid">
      <div class="lesson">
        <h3>A top-30 pilot lies about the long tail.</h3>
        <p>
          Testing a new source on the 30 most famous participants gave a 17% hit rate; running the full 698-person cohort yielded 0.7%. Pilots must sample the middle and bottom of the roster, never the head.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson">
        <h3>Identity found is not content found.</h3>
        <p>
          Early passes marked anyone with a verified VK profile as <code>enriched</code>, hiding 581 verified people who still had zero works. Splitting identity queues from project-harvest queues closed the gap.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson">
        <h3>Never run destructive deletes on scraped data.</h3>
        <p>
          When a Tilda results parser mistook participation rows for projects and wrote 1,004 rows, pre-flight backups and soft-delete pointers (<code>merged_into</code>) allowed an instant, audited rollback within the same session.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lesson">
        <h3>Naive batch APIs shift array indices.</h3>
        <p>
          Batch user lookups silently drop deleted accounts and return shorter arrays, shifting every subsequent profile onto the wrong person. All batch scripts were rewritten to return explicit <code>(query, record)</code> pairs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="delivery" aria-labelledby="delivery-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="delivery-title">Delivery shipped as live workbooks, an anonymized database, and an encrypted deck.</h2>
      <p>
        Personal-data rules shaped the final mile as strictly as the scraping: the master database with raw contacts, birth dates, and search logs remained on the local machine, while every deliverable was generated from a clean, contract-checked anonymized replica.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <div class="gate-flow">
        <div class="flow-node">
          <h3>Anonymized SQLite</h3>
          <p>Rebuilt without phones, emails, birth dates, or internal logs. Verified by an automated check for zero FK violations and schema compliance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true"><span>build</span><b>→</b></div>
        <div class="flow-node">
          <h3>Analytical workbooks</h3>
          <p>Generated via <code>openpyxl</code> with executive summary matrices, 3 live dashboard charts, a 2,449-row cinema registry, top box-office sheets, and 72 award records.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true"><span>encrypt</span><b>→</b></div>
        <div class="flow-node is-accent">
          <h3>Password-gated deck</h3>
          <p>29 slides in Reveal.js 5.1 with custom SVG charts, encrypted with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 (600,000 iterations) and AES-256-CBC for in-browser WebCrypto decryption.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Before shipping, an automated verification script cross-checked the SQLite views, the Excel summary tabs, and the presentation slides to eliminate discrepancies between period counts and career totals.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="stack" aria-labelledby="stack-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="stack-title">What runs underneath.</h2>
    </div>

    <dl class="stack-spec">
      <div>
        <dt>Python 3.12 + SQLite 3.45 + FTS5</dt>
        <dd>585 scripts (54,832 lines), master database (12 tables, 11 views, 7,356 audit events), and 20 local FTS5 source mirrors with sub-millisecond lookup.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>ijson, xml.etree.iterparse, gzip/csv</dt>
        <dd>Constant-memory streaming parsers for multi-gigabyte dumps (IMDb 102.6M rows, Kinopoisk 1.06M titles, Discogs 10.28M artists, TMDB 5.05M names, Steam 149K games).</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Playwright, MCP Extension, Crawl4AI, Scrapling</dt>
        <dd>Browser automation, live authenticated Chrome session relay for 401/captcha bypass, coordinate-based Tilda parsing, and Next.js state JWT extraction.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Exa, Apify, VKScript, Maigret, GHunt</dt>
        <dd>Semantic web search for hard blind spots, cloud actors for social batches, 25-user VKScript loops, nickname pivoting, and account triangulation.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>PyMuPDF (fitz) + openpyxl</dt>
        <dd>High-speed PDF table extraction (340 pages/s on grant registries, two-column spatial almanac parsing) and automated Excel workbooks with live charts and data bars.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Reveal.js 5.1 + WebCrypto AES-256-CBC</dt>
        <dd>Self-contained 29-slide analytical deck with inlined CSS assertions, PBKDF2 (600k iterations) payload encryption, and live DOM style verification on GitHub Pages.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</div>

